index=network_traffic protocol=tcp
| stats count(eval(tcp_flags="SYN")) as syn_count
count(eval(tcp_flags="ACK")) as ack_count
by src_ip, dest_ip, span
| eval syn_ack_ratio = syn_count / (ack_count + 1)
| where syn_ack_ratio > 5

### Rule Description
This rule detects potential TCP SYN flood activity by identifying source and destination IP address pairs with a high ratio of SYN packets to ACK packets. 
A SYN-to-ACK ratio greater than 5 may indicate an unusually high number of connection initiation attempts compared with acknowledgements, which can be associated with SYN flooding or other suspicious connection patterns.
The search counts TCP packets with SYN and ACK flags for each source-destination pair, calculates the SYN-to-ACK ratio, and flags pairs where the ratio exceeds the configured threshold of 5.
Investigate the source and destination IP addresses, packet volumes, connection patterns, and traffic over time to determine whether the activity indicates a potential denial-of-service attack.

**Note:** A high SYN-to-ACK ratio alone does not confirm a SYN flood. Legitimate connection attempts, asymmetric traffic visibility, and incomplete packet capture can also produce elevated ratios. The rule also assumes that `span` is an available field; otherwise, a time bucket should be defined explicitly in the SPL.
